new data is showing the world's largest  tropical rainforest is shrinking  fast scientists have for years been  warning that the amazon is disappearing  at an alarming rate  especially in brazil it's the last  refuge for many endangered wildlife  and home to millions of species some of  them still  unidentified and now research shows that  the deforestation of the brazilian  amazon  rose for the fourth consecutive month in  june hitting a record high  in the first six months of this year  alone it went up by  17 more than 3  500 square kilometers of amazon have  been cleared already  that's almost three times the size of  the city of rio de janeiro  many blame the government  this continues to happen because of the  government's environmental policy  encouraged by the national congress at  april's climate change summit hosted by  us president joe biden brazil's  president jaya bolsonaro pledged to  eliminate the illegal logging of the  amazon in the next few years  i'm determined that we will achieve our  climate neutrality by 2050  10 years ahead of our previous target  but satellite imagery suggests otherwise  and environmentalists say since  bolsonaro took office in 2019  the destruction has been happening at a  rapid rate  as institutions and legislations to  protect the rainforest and its  indigenous people  are increasingly marginalized  earlier this month and after weeks of  protests brazil's supreme court  postponed a ruling backed by the farming  sector  that would limit recognition of  indigenous lands  there's an attempt to pass laws in the  national congress with bowl 490  that takes away the territorial rights  of indigenous people  it's a signal to loggers and illegal  farmers on the rainforest grounds  that are willing to commit crimes from  the invasion of indigenous territories  deforestation and setting fires  scientists say the protection of the  amazon is vital to curbing climate  change  because of the vast amount of greenhouse  gases that the forest absorbs  june however had the biggest number of  forest fires since 2007  and that's only a fraction of those seen  annually during the peak of drought  season in august and september  and now there are fears the worst is yet  to come  al jazeera
 

